\n| ... | 1961 | 1962 | 1963 | 1964 | ... | 1965 |\\n\\n| 1966 | 1967 | 1968 | ... | 1969 | 1970 | 1971 |\\n\\n| 1972 | ... \",\"document\",[]],[\"s2148\",\"d2\",0,214,\"---\\ntitle: Chronology\\ndate: 2023-01-17\\n---\\nWelcome to our Midway Island Chronology page, a comprehensive online resource dedicated to unfolding the rich sequence of events that have shaped Midway Island's history. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s2149\",\"d2\",214,454,\"From its discovery in the 19th century to its pivotal role in World War II and its current status as a wildlife refuge, our Chronology offers a detailed and engaging timeline of this small yet significant atoll in the North Pacific Ocean.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s2150\",\"d2\",454,853,\"Perfect for historians, educators, students, or anyone fascinated by the intersection of nature and history, our Midway Island Chronology page is a gateway to understanding the enduring legacy of this remarkable island.\\n\\nChronology of Events for Midway Island\\n\\n►July 5, 1859, Tuesday – Captain N.C. Brooks of the Hawaiian Bark Gambia discovers the Island. Islands were named \\\"Middlebrook Islands\\\".\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s2151\",\"d2\",853,1205,\"►August 28, 1867, Wednesday – Captain William Reynolds of the USS Lackawanna takes possession of the Island for the United States. Midway becomes the first offshore islands annexed by the U.S. government.\\n\\n►January 28, 1869, Thursday – The Navy adopts the name Midway Islands, according to a report submitted to the Senate Committee on Naval Affairs.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s2152\",\"d2\",1205,1581,\"►November 16, 1886, Tuesday – The schooner General Siegel was shipwrecked at Midway during a storm.\\n\\n►February 3, 1888, Friday – The British bark Wandering Minstrel was shipwrecked in Wells Harbor, during a storm.\\n\\n►January 17, 1899, Tuesday – The USS Bennington took possession of Wake Island, with the intention of having it used as a cable station between Midway and Guam. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s2606\",\"d3\",0,379,\"---\\ntitle: List of late-night American network TV programs - Wikipedia\\ndate: 2005-09-24\\n---\\nLate-night television in the United States is the block of television programming intended for broadcast after 11:00 p.m. and usually through 2:00 a.m. Eastern and Pacific Time (ET/PT), but which informally can include programs aired as late as the designated overnight graveyard slot.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s2607\",\"d3\",379,757,\"By definition, late night programming begins on the Big Three television networks (ABC, NBC and CBS) at or shortly before 11:35 p.m. ET/PT, after the conclusion of local late-evening newscasts on their owned-and-operated and affiliated stations; late night programming on other broadcast networks, including Fox and PBS, and cable television channels start at 11:00 p.m. ET/PT. \",\"document\",[]],[\"s2608\",\"d3\",757,1038,\"Some streaming services (such as Netflix, Hulu and Amazon Prime Video) have ventured into the late-night talk format at various times in recent years, though these programs are in-format-only, given that episodes are often released before the start of the designated time period.\\n\\n\",\"document\",[]],[\"s2609\",\"d3\",1038,1417,\"The following is a list of programs that are currently airing or have previously aired during the late night daypart on American television networks and streaming services.\\n\\nCurrent\\n\\n**Table 1**\\n\\n| Network | Program title | Format | Duration | Days | Time (ET) | Currenthost(s)/anchor(s) | Debut | Length of run |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| ABC | Jimmy Kimmel Live! \",\"document\",[]],[\"s2610\",\"d3\",1417,1736,\"| Talk show | 60 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Jimmy Kimmel | January 26, 2003 | |\\n| ABC | Nightline | Newsmagazine | 30 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Byron Pitts,Juju Chang | March 24, 1980 | |\\n| ABC | World News Now | Overnight newscast | 90 minutes | Monday–Friday | | Andrew Dymburt,Rhiannon Ally | January 6, 19